**Theorem 4.8 (fixed same-image quantitative matching of actual interval sums).** Given an actual natural map $f:I_A\to I_B$ between arbitrary finite positive-length interval families over an arbitrary field, there exist one positive-length image family $C$, a natural isomorphism $\operatorname{im}f\cong I_C$, the actual transported factors $e:I_A\twoheadrightarrow I_C$ and $m:I_C\hookrightarrow I_B$ with $me=f$, sorted source/image birth-fiber and image/target death-fiber enumerations, and ordinal-preserving embeddings $s:C\hookrightarrow A$ and $t:C\hookrightarrow B$. All these witnesses are fixed before the choice of independent nonnegative parameters $\eta_K,\eta_C$. Whenever the actual conditions $\ker f_r\subseteq\ker(I_A(r\to r+\eta_K))$ and $\operatorname{im}(I_B(r\to r+\eta_C))\subseteq\operatorname{im}f_{r+\eta_C}$ hold at every real $r$, those same embeddings satisfy

$$b_{t(c)}\le b_c=b_{s(c)}\le b_{t(c)}+\eta_C,\qquad d_c=d_{t(c)}\le d_{s(c)}.$$

For finite paired source and image deaths, $d_{s(c)}\le d_c+\eta_K$; essential source death is equivalent to essential image death on paired occurrences. Every source occurrence longer than $\eta_K$ and every target occurrence longer than $\eta_C$ is covered, as are all essentials on both sides. Every unmatched finite source or target length is at most its respective parameter. A zero kernel parameter forces full source coverage and equal deaths, and a zero cokernel parameter forces full target coverage and equal births. Empty occurrence families, zero maps, repeated intervals and arbitrary real endpoints are allowed.

**Proof.** The common finite grid of all finite endpoints realizes the image of the same $f$ as a finite diagram. The natural finite-diagram classification gives $C$ and its image isomorphism. Invertible naturality squares transport images using the image functor. Choose the two ordered embeddings once, keeping their ordinal equations. The actual factorization transfers the range condition to $m$ and the kernel condition to $e$. Apply Theorems 4.6 and 4.7 with precisely these fixed image enumerations and embeddings. Their coverage bounds imply the unmatched-length estimates. This theorem concerns actual supported interval-sum maps; transfer to arbitrary finite-diagram extensions and the exact interleaving converse are additional formal conclusions.

